  "query_preview": "../Friday through Monday/...A low pressure system \noriginating from a mid-level North Pacific trough will move along \nthe Alaska Peninsula and into the Gulf of Alaska this weekend. \nReinforced by a stronger system behind it, it will produce \nprolonged rainfall from Saturday into early next week, fueled by \ntropical moisture and weak but continuous divergent flow aloft. \nModels continuing to favor landfall along the north-central coast\nnear Cordova. The system could bring moderate to heavy rain, with\nthe highest totals expected along the northeast Gulf coast and \nhigher terrain, though most of the region will be impacted. Warm \nadvection seems apparent through many levels of the atmosphere \nalong this system, which will allow for a very slight warming in \ntemperatures, but not expecting much of a difference due to \npersistent cloud coverage across the region this weekend.\n\nClimatological guidance is trending higher, though values remain \nwithin seasonal norms. The NE Gulf Coast may see locally heavy,\nlong duration rainfall instead of showery bursts due to limited \ncold air aloft and minimal convective activity. For the heaviest \nperiod (Saturday evening to Sunday evening), 24-hour QPF averages \n1.5-3 inches, with up to 4-5 inches at higher elevations. GEFS and\nEuro ENS indicate PWATs around 175% of normal. The GEPS is \ntrending more aggressive, while the GEFS and Euro ENS align more \non timing and intensity. Stronger winds and building seas are also\nincreasingly likely along the outer coast and northern inner \nchannels ahead of the low. The active pattern will persist into \nnext week as the following Bering Sea low reinforces onshore flow,\nthough rain chances should taper off by mid week.",
